<section xml:id="section-double-integrals-polar-form" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Double Integrals in Polar Form</title>

<p>
In the previous session, we talked about a new coordinate system known
as polar coordinates. Today, we will use polar coordinates to calculate
double integrals. As you will see, this set of coordinates can
sometimes simplify our calculations. In Cartesian coordinates, double
integrals are of the form <xref ref="eq-double-integral-def"/>,
</p>

<me>
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dx\,dy = \iint_R f(x,y)\,dA.
</me>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-double-integral">
<title>How Does a Double Integral Look in Polar Coordinates?</title>

<p>
To answer this question, assume that our region of integration is
</p>

<me>
R = \{(r,\theta) : a \le r \le b,\ \alpha \le \theta \le \beta\},
</me>

<p>
which is shown on the left in
<xref ref="fig-polar-rectangles-prefigure"/>. We divide this region
into <em>polar rectangles</em> as shown on the right. Then, we
calculate the area <m>\Delta A_i</m> of a typical polar rectangle
</p>

<me>
R_{ij} = \{(r,\theta) : r_{i-1} \le r \le r_i,\
\theta_{j-1} \le \theta \le \theta_j\},
</me>

<p>
Compute this area. (The area of a sector of a circle with radius
<m>r</m> and central angle <m>\theta</m> is <m>\tfrac12 r^2\theta</m>;
the polar rectangle is the difference of two sectors. Factor the
difference of squares and write the result in terms of
<m>\Delta r = r_i - r_{i-1}</m>, <m>\Delta\theta</m>, and the average
radius <m>r_i^* = \tfrac12(r_i + r_{i-1})</m>. Then write the Riemann
sum for <m>\iint_R f\,dA</m> with these cells and pass to the limit.)
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the area of a polar rectangle and the polar form of the double integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>

<assemblage xml:id="assemblage-polar-double-integral">
<title>Double Integrals in Polar Coordinates</title>
<p>
The area of a polar rectangle with average radius <m>r_i^*</m> is
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-polar-area-element" number="yes">
\Delta A_i = r_i^*\,\Delta r\,\Delta\theta,
</mrow>
</md>
so that <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m>, and
<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-polar-double-integral" number="yes">
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA = \iint_R f(r\cos\theta,\ r\sin\theta)\,r\,dr\,d\theta.
</mrow>
</md>
</p>
</assemblage>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-rectangles-prefigure">
