<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-volume-example">
<title>A Volume Computed in Polar Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-polar-volume">
<title>Example III</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find the volume of the solid that lies under the paraboloid
<m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>, above the <m>xy</m>-plane, and inside the
cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2x</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,15);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,14.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Example III.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

<!-- The figure fig-shifted-disk-prefigure (the base disk of Example III)
     is hidden at the instructor's request (Oct 2026); it is in the
     completed notes. -->

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-volume-tikz">
<caption>
The solid of <xref ref="example-polar-volume"/>: the region under the
paraboloid <m>z = x^2 + y^2</m>, above the <m>xy</m>-plane, and inside
the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 2x</m>.
</caption>
<image width="70%">
<shortdescription>
A paraboloid rising inside an off-center vertical cylinder above the plane.
</shortdescription>
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a vertical circular cylinder of
radius one centered on the line x equals one, y equals zero. Inside
the cylinder, the paraboloid z equals x squared plus y squared rises
from the origin; the solid between the x y plane and the paraboloid,
inside the cylinder, is the region whose volume is computed. Dashed
lines mark the base circle.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={125}{22},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=2.7, ymin=-1.5, ymax=1.6, zmin=0, zmax=4.9,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- base disk boundary r = 2 cos(theta) ----
    \addplot3[oivermillion, line width=1.4pt, domain=0:360, samples=97,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1 + cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, 0);
    % ---- cylinder wall ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=interp,
              colormap={softc}{color=(oiblue!8!white) color=(oiblue!30!white)},
              opacity=0.30, fill opacity=0.30,
              domain=0:360, samples=49,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=2,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({1 + cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {y*4.4});
    % ---- paraboloid z = r^2 over the disk: parametrize by s (angle), t ----
    % point (1 + t cos s, t sin s), z = (1+t cos s)^2 + (t sin s)^2
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!45,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!15!white) color=(oiblue!50!white)},
              opacity=0.65, fill opacity=0.65,
              domain=0:360, samples=37,
              y domain=0:1, samples y=13,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      ({1 + y*cos(x)}, {y*sin(x)},
       {(1 + y*cos(x))^2 + (y*sin(x))^2});
    % ---- rim: intersection of paraboloid and cylinder ----
    \addplot3[oiblue, line width=1.4pt, domain=0:360, samples=97,
        samples y=0, forget plot]
      ({1 + cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {(1 + cos(x))^2 + (sin(x))^2});
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.98)
      {$z = x^2 + y^2$};
    \node[oivermillion, font=\small, anchor=south east]
      at (axis description cs:0.98,0.02) {base circle $x^2 + y^2 = 2x$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-polar-gaussian-example">
<title>An Integral That Needs Polar Coordinates</title>

<example xml:id="example-polar-gaussian">
<title>Example IV</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\iint_R e^{x^2+y^2}\,dx\,dy</m>, where <m>R</m> is the
unit disk centered at the origin.
</p>

<p>
<em>Hint:</em> Notice that you cannot solve this integral using
Cartesian coordinates.
</p>
<p>
<em>Solution.</em>
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,8);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,7.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for Example IV.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</example>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsec-polar-questions">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<p>
Each question below asks for a <em>setup</em> in polar coordinates. Recall
from <xref ref="eq-polar-cartesian"/> that <m>x = r\cos\theta</m>,
<m>y = r\sin\theta</m>, and from <xref ref="eq-polar-area-element"/>
that <m>dA = r\,dr\,d\theta</m>, so that the double integral takes the
polar form <xref ref="eq-polar-double-integral"/>.
</p>

<p>
The work is almost entirely in the limits: which values of <m>\theta</m>
sweep the region, and for each such <m>\theta</m>, between which two
values of <m>r</m> does the ray at that angle enter and leave the region.
Two of the figures below carry a slider for <m>\theta</m> so you can watch
the ray move.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-three-areas">
<title>Three areas</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up <term>three</term> (not more than three) integrals that correspond
to the following areas graphed in <xref ref="fig-polar-two-areas"/>:
</p>
<ol>
<li><p>the red-colored area,</p></li>
<li><p>the blue-colored area,</p></li>
<li><p>the sum of the areas in (1) and (2).</p></li>
</ol>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the three area integrals.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-two-areas">
<caption>
Figure (1): two regions between the circles <m>r=1</m> and <m>r=2</m>,
cut apart by the vertical line <m>x=1</m> and swept by the angles
<m>0 \le \theta \le \pi/3</m>. The line <m>y=\sqrt3\,x</m> is the ray
<m>\theta = \pi/3</m>, and it meets <m>x=1</m> exactly on the outer
circle.
</caption>
<image width="90%">
<shortdescription>
Two semicircles, a slanted line, and two vertical lines bounding two shaded regions.
</shortdescription>
<prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-polar-two-areas">
<diagram dimensions="(500,290)" margins="8">
<definition>inner(t) = (cos(t), sin(t))</definition>
<definition>outer(t) = (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t))</definition>
<coordinates bbox="(-2.45,-0.5,2.75,2.4)">
<polygon at="pa-red" closed="yes" fill="#DBBBA6" stroke="none"
    points="((1.0000,0.0000),(0.9996,0.0268),(0.9986,0.0537),(0.9968,0.0805),(0.9942,0.1072),(0.9910,0.1339),(0.9871,0.1604),(0.9824,0.1869),(0.9770,0.2132),(0.9709,0.2393),(0.9642,0.2653),(0.9567,0.2911),(0.9485,0.3167),(0.9397,0.3420),(0.9302,0.3671),(0.9200,0.3920),(0.9091,0.4165),(0.8976,0.4408),(0.8855,0.4647),(0.8727,0.4883),(0.8592,0.5116),(0.8452,0.5345),(0.8305,0.5570),(0.8153,0.5791),(0.7994,0.6007),(0.7830,0.6220),(0.7660,0.6428),(0.7485,0.6631),(0.7304,0.6830),(0.7118,0.7023),(0.6927,0.7212),(0.6731,0.7395),(0.6530,0.7573),(0.6324,0.7746),(0.6114,0.7913),(0.5900,0.8074),(0.5681,0.8230),(0.5458,0.8379),(0.5231,0.8523),(0.5000,0.8660),(1.0000,1.7321),(1.0000,0.0000))"/>
<polygon at="pa-blue" closed="yes" fill="#D1F0FF" stroke="none"
    points="((1.0000,0.0000),(2.0000,0.0000),(2.0000,0.0000),(1.9993,0.0537),(1.9971,0.1074),(1.9935,0.1609),(1.9885,0.2144),(1.9820,0.2677),(1.9741,0.3208),(1.9648,0.3737),(1.9540,0.4263),(1.9419,0.4786),(1.9283,0.5306),(1.9134,0.5822),(1.8971,0.6333),(1.8794,0.6840),(1.8603,0.7343),(1.8400,0.7839),(1.8182,0.8330),(1.7952,0.8816),(1.7709,0.9294),(1.7453,0.9767),(1.7185,1.0232),(1.6904,1.0689),(1.6611,1.1139),(1.6306,1.1581),(1.5989,1.2015),(1.5661,1.2440),(1.5321,1.2856),(1.4970,1.3262),(1.4609,1.3660),(1.4237,1.4047),(1.3854,1.4424),(1.3462,1.4791),(1.3060,1.5147),(1.2649,1.5492),(1.2228,1.5826),(1.1799,1.6149),(1.1361,1.6460),(1.0915,1.6759),(1.0461,1.7046),(1.0000,1.7321))"/>
<axes at="pa-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
<parametric-curve at="pa-inner" function="inner" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#555555" thickness="2"/>
<parametric-curve at="pa-outer" function="outer" domain="(0,pi)"
    stroke="#555555" thickness="2"/>
<line at="pa-ray" endpoints="((-0.28,-0.485),(1.28,2.217))"
    stroke="#333333" thickness="2"/>
<line at="pa-x1" endpoints="((1,-0.4),(1,2.3))"
    stroke="#777777" thickness="1.5" dash="5 4"/>
<line at="pa-x2" endpoints="((2,-0.4),(2,2.3))"
    stroke="#777777" thickness="1.5" dash="5 4"/>
<point at="pa-C" p="(0.5,0.866)" fill="black" alignment="nw"/>
<point at="pa-A" p="(1,1.732)" fill="black"/>
<label at="pa-A-label" anchor="(0.92,1.80)" alignment="nw">
<m>\left(1,\sqrt3\right)</m>
</label>
<point at="pa-E" p="(1,0)" fill="black" alignment="sw"/>
<point at="pa-F" p="(2,0)" fill="black" alignment="se"/>
<label at="pa-ray-label" anchor="(1.30,2.21)" alignment="e"
    color="#333333"><m>\theta = \pi/3</m></label>
<label at="pa-inner-label" anchor="(-0.85,0.95)" alignment="nw"
    color="#555555"><m>r=1</m></label>
<label at="pa-outer-label" anchor="(-1.55,1.45)" alignment="nw"
    color="#555555"><m>r=2</m></label>
<label at="pa-x1-label" anchor="(1,-0.42)" alignment="s"
    color="#777777"><m>x=1</m></label>
<label at="pa-x2-label" anchor="(2,-0.42)" alignment="s"
    color="#777777"><m>x=2</m></label>
<label at="pa-red-label" anchor="(0.86,0.72)" alignment="center"><m>1</m></label>
<label at="pa-blue-label" anchor="(1.45,0.45)" alignment="center"><m>2</m></label>
</coordinates>
<annotations>
<annotation ref="figure" text="Two concentric semicircles of radius one and two centered at the origin, the line y equals the square root of three times x, and the vertical lines x equals one and x equals two. Between the two circles and below the line, region one is shaded red: it lies between the unit circle and the line x equals one. Region two is shaded blue: it lies between the line x equals one and the circle of radius two, above the x axis. The line through the origin meets the vertical line x equals one at the point one, square root of three, which is also on the outer circle.">
  <annotation ref="pa-red" text="Region one, shaded red, bounded by the unit circle on the inside, the vertical line x equals one on the outside, the x axis below and the line y equals root three x above."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-blue" text="Region two, shaded blue, bounded by the vertical line x equals one on the inside, the circle of radius two on the outside, and the x axis below."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-inner" text="The circle r equals one."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-outer" text="The circle r equals two."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-ray" text="The line y equals root three x, that is the ray theta equals pi over three."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-x1" text="The vertical line x equals one, which in polar coordinates is r equals secant theta."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-x2" text="The vertical line x equals two."/>
  <annotation ref="pa-A" text="The point one, square root of three, where the ray, the line x equals one and the outer circle all meet."/>
</annotation>
</annotations>
</diagram>
</prefigure>
</image>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-rose-mass">
<title>Mass of a piece of a rose</title>
<statement>
<p>
Region <m>R</m> is the region <term>inside</term>
<m>r=\cos(3\theta)</m> and <term>inside</term> <m>r=\frac12</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-polar-rose-circle"/>. Set up a double integral for
computing the mass of a planar object that occupies region <m>R</m> and
whose density is given by
<m>\rho(x,y)=\dfrac{1}{\sqrt{x^2+y^2}}</m>.
</p>
<p>
<em>Hint</em>: note that as <m>\theta</m> varies, you may need to modify
the limits of <m>r</m> accordingly.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the mass integral.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-rose-circle">
<caption>
Figure (2): the rose <m>r=\cos(3\theta)</m> and the circle
<m>r=\frac12</m>. Drag the slider to turn the ray of angle
<m>\theta</m>; the dots mark where that ray crosses each curve, which is
what fixes the limits of <m>r</m>.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="int-polar-rose" platform="javascript" source="polar-widget.js"
             width="100%" aspect="10:11">
<slate surface="html">
<div data-widget="rose" style="text-align:center;
     font-family: var(--font-body, 'Open Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif);">
<canvas width="460" height="460"
        style="width:460px; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;"></canvas>
<div style="margin-top:0.6em; display:flex; align-items:center;
            justify-content:center; gap:0.7em; flex-wrap:wrap;">
<label for="rose-theta" style="font-family: var(--font-headings, 'PT Serif', Times, serif);
       font-style:italic; font-size:1.2em;">&#952;</label>
<input id="rose-theta" type="range" min="0" max="48" value="0" step="1"
       style="width:60%; max-width:340px;"/>
</div>
<div class="pw-readout" style="margin-top:0.5em; line-height:1.5;"></div>
</div>
</slate>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to change <m>\theta</m>, which moves in steps of
<m>\pi/24</m>. The readout under the picture gives
<m>\cos(3\theta)</m> for the current ray, and the upper limit of
<m>r</m> for the region inside both curves: it is <m>\frac12</m> while
the ray is near the middle of a petal, and <m>\cos(3\theta)</m> near the
edges of a petal.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-cylinder-sphere">
<title>The ball and the cylinder again, in polar</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up a double integral (in polar coordinates) for computing the volume
of the region bounded by the cylinder <m>x^2+(y-1)^2=1</m> and the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m>. See <xref ref="fig-ws-cylinder-sphere"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,12);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,11.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the volume integral in polar coordinates.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="q-polar-cardioid-overlap">
<title>The overlap of two cardioids</title>
<statement>
<p>
Set up integral(s) for computing the area of the <term>overlap</term> of
the two curves <m>r(\theta)=1+\cos\theta</m> and
<m>r(\theta)=1+\sin\theta</m>. See
<xref ref="fig-polar-cardioids"/>.
</p>

<sidebyside width="97%">
<image>
<latex-image>
\begin{tikzpicture}
\draw[rounded corners=8pt, draw=gray!55, thick] (0,0) rectangle (15,10);
\node[anchor=north west, text=gray!60, font=\footnotesize\itshape]
  at (0.25,9.75) {workspace};
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
<shortdescription>Blank workspace for the area of the overlap.</shortdescription>
</image>
</sidebyside>
</statement>
</exercise>

<figure xml:id="fig-polar-cardioids">
<caption>
Figure (4): the cardioids <m>r=1+\cos\theta</m> and
<m>r=1+\sin\theta</m>. Drag the slider to turn the ray of angle
<m>\theta</m>; the overlap is what the ray reaches before it leaves the
first of the two curves.
</caption>
<interactive xml:id="int-polar-cardioids" platform="javascript" source="polar-widget.js"
             width="100%" aspect="10:11">
<slate surface="html">
<div data-widget="cardioids" style="text-align:center;
     font-family: var(--font-body, 'Open Sans', Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif);">
<canvas width="460" height="460"
        style="width:460px; max-width:100%; height:auto; border:1px solid #ddd;"></canvas>
<div style="margin-top:0.6em; display:flex; align-items:center;
            justify-content:center; gap:0.7em; flex-wrap:wrap;">
<label for="card-theta" style="font-family: var(--font-headings, 'PT Serif', Times, serif);
       font-style:italic; font-size:1.2em;">&#952;</label>
<input id="card-theta" type="range" min="0" max="48" value="0" step="1"
       style="width:60%; max-width:340px;"/>
</div>
<div class="pw-readout" style="margin-top:0.5em; line-height:1.5;"></div>
</div>
</slate>
<instructions>
<p>
Drag the slider to change <m>\theta</m>, which moves in steps of
<m>\pi/24</m>. The readout gives both radii for the current ray and the smaller of the two, which is the upper limit of
<m>r</m> for the overlap. Watch it swap from one curve to the other as
the ray passes <m>\theta = \frac{\pi}{4}</m> and
<m>\theta = \frac{5\pi}{4}</m>.
</p>
</instructions>
</interactive>
</figure>

</subsection>

</section>
